against the mutant logs is the order witness.  Not edited after the table.

BASELINE NAMED: GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c as it stands uncommitted on b78bb0f
(md5 294222357ca13dfe632edbcfeb421ad6), cmp-clean into /tmp/gxopus_p7, rebuilt there.
INSTRUMENT: frozen copy of GXEMUL-SEC/regress/pcc2_width_probe.py, md5 ef3f43b4... --
which is part 6b's corrected instrument, so scores are comparable with part 6b.

SECTION O1 -- THE CLAIM UNDER TEST.
The contract says the guard's OPERAND SPACE is "CLOSED AS A CLASS", enumerating fields
(pcctwo_reg[relative_addr], warned, pending_timer1/2 + the TxICR INT bit) and naming a
carrying row for each.  The enumeration is PER FIELD.  The class-closure amendment demands
the full space "from the function's own inputs" -- i.e. per (field, ARM) CELL.  The
pend1/pend2 rows hold pending_timer* non-initial at exactly ONE of the twelve arms (TxICR).
Both pass-6 measurements (Opus `&& d->pending_timer1_interrupts == 0', Fable
`&& !d->pending_timer2_interrupts') were also at TxICR.

MUTANT M1_PENDT2_ALL12: append `&& d->pending_timer2_interrupts == 0' to ALL TWELVE width
guards (GENCTL, VECBASE, PSCALEADJ, TxCTL, TxICR, SCCERR, SCCICR, SCCTX, SCCRX, SCSIICR,
IPL, MASK).

PREDICTION  M1_PENDT2_ALL12   157/159   RED = pend2 decl, pend2 rb   (exactly two rows)
  Reasoning: pending_timer2_interrupts is written ONLY by pcc_timer_tick (SIGALRM), and only
  the `pend2' session ever holds it non-zero.  That session's ONLY wide write is the guest
  st.h at T2ICR.  So at the eleven arms other than TxICR the added conjunct is TRUE at every
  decision point in all 159 rows and the guard behaves exactly as shipped.
  The TxICR half is the SELF-CONTROL: it must reproduce pass 6's M_PENDT2_TXICR score and
  red-set byte for byte (157/159, `pend2 decl,pend2 rb'), which proves the edit is live in
  the binary.
  IF THE SCORE IS 157/159 WITH THAT RED SET, then eleven of twelve cells for this field are
  UNCOVERED and "closed as a class" is measured false.  If it is lower, some row I have not
  identified covers another arm and the closure claim is stronger than I think.

MUTANT M2_IPLZERO_ALL12 (only if time permits): append `&& d->pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_IPL] == 0'
to all twelve guards.
PREDICTION  M2_IPLZERO_ALL12  155/159   RED = pend1 decl, pend1 rb, pend2 decl, pend2 rb
  Reasoning: reassert_interrupts() sets reg[IPL] = intlevel = (TxICR & PCC2_TTIRQ_IL) = 1 in
  both pend sessions and leaves it 0 in every other session (no interrupt source is armed).
  At the ten non-TxICR arms the conjunct is inert.  The contract discloses this shape only
  "at that guard" (IPL) and only with a "one message" consequence; at GENCTL/MASK/VECBASE the
  consequence is a LANDED NEIGHBOUR LANE, and at GENCTL with lane 0xdd/0x44 it is a HOST KILL
  via pcc2c040kill.

SECTION O2 -- STATIC FINDINGS ALREADY DERIVED FROM THE TREE AND THE ROUND'S OWN ARTIFACTS,
recorded here BEFORE the table so their order is witnessed too:
 O2a  `rb4 genctl' and `rb8 genctl' kill by HOST DEATH, not by readback value.  The 4-byte
      pattern's low lane is 0xdd and the 8-byte pattern's is 0x44; BOTH have PCC2_C040 (0x04)
      SET, and GENCTL's body calls reassert_interrupts(), whose first test is
      `if (reg[GENCTL] & PCC2_C040) { fatal(); exit(1); }'.  The probe still PRINTS
      "NOROW  pcc2c040kill -- ... every GENCTL lane here keeps that bit clear".  MEASURED in
      the round's own logs: part6b/M_W4FALL_ALL.out lines 85-86 read `died' for both rows,
      while part6/M_W4FALL_ALL.out line 86 reads `0x33' for rb8 genctl (0x33 & 0x04 == 0).
      The 0x33 -> 0x44 value change made to de-vacuate rb8 t2ctl/t2icr SILENTLY CONVERTED
      rb8 genctl's channel from a value to a death.
 O2b  PRETABLE_pass6b Section 13's landed-lane enumeration is wrong at SCCTX: it says
      "SCCTX 0x44" but SCCTX's arm is `= idata & 0x1f', so the landed lane is 0x04.
      Section 12's len-4 enumeration says "GENCTL/MASK/SCC*: 0xdd"; SCCICR and SCCTX are
      `& 0x1f', so their landed lane is 0x1d.  Both harmless (still != preset), both wrong.
 O2c  The contract's "only VECBASE 0x0f and PSCALEADJ 0xdf are non-zero at init" is FALSE:
      dev_pcc2_init sets d->pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_CHIPID] = PCC2_ID = 0x20
      (mvme_pcctworeg.h:57).  A guard conditioned on reg[CHIPID] being NONZERO is therefore
      NOT killed by `latch all' -- it is inert and escapes all 159 rows.
 O2d  The operand enumeration omits `relative_addr', which the TxICR body reads four times.
      (Covered in fact by `rb t1icr'/`op t1icr dcl'/`pend1 *' and `rb t2icr'/`pend2 *', so a
      records gap, not a coverage gap.)
 O2e  reassert_interrupts() is a CALLEE of seven of the twelve guarded bodies (GENCTL, TxICR,
      SCCICR, SCCTX, SCCRX, SCSIICR, MASK) and reads reg[GENCTL], reg[T1ICR], reg[T2ICR],
      reg[SCCTX], reg[SCCRX], reg[SCSIICR], reg[IPL], reg[MASK], pending_timer1/2 and
      cur_int_vec is WRITTEN not read.  The contract's enumeration mentions none of this and
      does not say whether callee reads are in or out of closure.  (The cur_int_vec claim
      itself is TRUE: it is only ever written there.)
